[rexglue-native] SDK 0311, released in setup tool 2.31.0 (2026-11-04): Vulkan async submit. New setting
vulkan_async_submit (default off, read at start). Off, nothing changes.

With it on, the GPU emulation thread closes each submission as before (fence, submission number,
semaphores, bookkeeping) and hands the recorded stream (swapped, not copied) to one submit thread through a
3-job ring; that thread replays it into the command buffer and calls vkQueueSubmit. The GPU thread waits for
it only before waiting for a fence, the presenter's use of the guest output image, the memexport readback
poll, occlusion query waits, and shutdown. r2 removes the wait at the swap (with one submission a frame the
frame's only submission closes there); r3 runs the presenter's end-of-frame work on the GPU thread, never on
the submit thread, where its waits could stall the ring (seen as stalls with ~17 submissions a frame and as a
deadlock with the stock setting vulkan_submit_on_primary_buffer_end=false, one submission a frame).

This file is three drafts folded into one diff (applies after 0309):
  0305    vulkan async submit       rexglue-native/0305.patch                                 46428b7b
  0305 r2 no drain at the swap      rexglue-native/0305r2.patch                               ae44150e
  0305 r3 presenter tail fix        drafts/0305-r3-fix-tail-deadlock.UNTESTED.patch           c1009280
The diff is git's diff between the tree with everything below it applied and the tree with all three
applied (rexglue-native/rel3/release/0311-folded.diff).

Tested by rexglue-native-test on the owner's PC (2026-11-03/04, 4-player split screen, ours alone, uncapped):
async on with one submission a frame is the cheapest per draw, 11-15 % less GPU emulation thread time a
draw than the defaults (mp_merv about +17 % fps); no deadlock; 0 startup hangs in 40 starts with async on
(dev/agents/rexglue-native/R4-SCALE-TIMING.md, NR9-ASYNC-SUBMIT-FLARE.md, NR10-ONE-SUBMIT-PER-FRAME.md).

